---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const sections = [
  {
    title: 'Universal taxes',
    description: 'No principles, no jargon. Five pieces of code that work for the first user and bite later.',
    cards: [
      { n: 1, title: 'Uniqueness lives in the database, not in valid?', principle: 'Database constraints', slug: 'tax-database-constraints' },
      { n: 2, title: 'External I/O belongs outside the transaction', principle: 'Transaction scope', slug: 'tax-transaction-scope' },
      { n: 3, title: ':destroy runs in Ruby, :delete_all runs in SQL', principle: 'Bulk operations in SQL', slug: 'tax-bulk-operations' },
      { n: 4, title: "JSON-as-text blocks the queries you'll need later", principle: 'Indexable storage', slug: 'tax-indexable-storage' },
      { n: 5, title: 'Authorization belongs in the query, not after', principle: 'Scoped queries', slug: 'tax-scoped-queries' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'SOLID, one card per letter',
    description: 'Each principle in SOLID, demonstrated through one tax that real teams have paid in production.',
    cards: [
      { n: 6, title: 'One class, one reason to change', principle: 'Single responsibility', slug: 'tax-single-responsibility' },
      { n: 7, title: "Adding a variant shouldn't mean editing every file", principle: 'Open/closed', slug: 'tax-open-closed' },
      { n: 8, title: "A subclass that breaks the parent's promises isn't really a subclass", principle: 'Liskov substitution', slug: 'tax-liskov-substitution' },
      { n: 9, title: 'Depend on the smallest interface that does the job', principle: 'Interface segregation', slug: 'tax-interface-segregation' },
      { n: 10, title: "Business logic shouldn't know which payment provider you use", principle: 'Dependency inversion', slug: 'tax-dependency-inversion' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Senior gotchas',
    description: 'Concurrency, retries, scaling, error handling, schema escape hatches. The taxes that don\'t fit cleanly into a SOLID slot.',
    cards: [
      { n: 11, title: "Read-modify-write isn't atomic", principle: 'Atomic operations', slug: 'tax-atomic-updates' },
      { n: 12, title: 'Rescue only what you can recover from', principle: 'Targeted error handling', slug: 'tax-exception-handling' },
      { n: 13, title: 'Background jobs must be safe to run twice', principle: 'Idempotency', slug: 'tax-idempotent-jobs' },
      { n: 14, title: 'User.all.each will eventually run out of memory', principle: 'Bounded memory iteration', slug: 'tax-batched-iteration' },
      { n: 15, title: 'update_all is a SQL escape hatch', principle: 'Performance shortcuts skip the framework', slug: 'tax-callback-bypass' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'DDD in Rails',
    description: 'Domain-driven design ideas applied in a Rails codebase. Anemic models, primitive obsession, leaky boundaries, missing events, and broken aggregates.',
    cards: [
      { n: 16, title: "Models that don't know their own rules", principle: 'Models own their own rules', slug: 'tax-anemic-models' },
      { n: 17, title: 'A phone number is more than a string', principle: 'Value objects', slug: 'tax-value-objects' },
      { n: 18, title: 'One model class serving four teams', principle: 'Bounded contexts', slug: 'tax-bounded-contexts' },
      { n: 19, title: 'When the controller knows about every downstream concern', principle: 'Domain events', slug: 'tax-domain-events' },
      { n: 20, title: 'Mutating a child without going through the parent breaks invariants', principle: 'Aggregate roots', slug: 'tax-aggregate-roots' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Spot the Tax — All Lessons" description="Twenty short Rails lessons. Each one shows code that works today and walks through what it'll cost you in six months. Pick a card to start." containerClass="max-w-5xl">
  <article class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-10">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Senior Track</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">Spot the Tax</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Twenty short Rails lessons. Each one shows a snippet of code that works today and walks you through what it'll cost in six months — under load, after a deploy, when the table grows. Look at the code, try to spot the tax yourself, then reveal the answer.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-6">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>How to use this page.</strong> Pick any lesson and start there — the lessons don't depend on each other. Each one takes about 5 minutes: look at the code, take a moment to think about the fix, then reveal the solution and the underlying principle. The principles are the part worth remembering.
      </p>
    </div>

    {sections.map((section, sectionIdx) => (
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-1">Section {sectionIdx + 1} of 4</p>
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">{section.title}</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-600 mt-2">{section.description}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          {section.cards.map((card) => (
            <a
              href={`/lessons/${card.slug}`}
              class="block bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-6 hover:shadow-md hover:border-ruby/30 transition-all group"
            >
              <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-bold mb-3">Card {card.n}</p>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 group-hover:text-ruby-dark mb-3 leading-snug" set:html={card.title}></h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">
                <span class="text-amber-500">💡</span>
                {card.principle}
              </p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}

    <div class="bg-ruby/5 border border-ruby/20 rounded-lg p-6">
      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        These lessons aren't a curriculum, they're a collection. The same five "I do that every week" recognitions, twenty times. Working through the deck once teaches you the principles. Coming back to the deck a few months later, when the same patterns show up in a code review, is when the senior reflex actually starts to form.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Back to Course</a>
      <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Senior Track · Spot the Tax · 20 lessons</span>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
